## Late acknowledgment of plan__inference-perf-oom-root-cause-found-20260813.md

Found this unprocessed during a handoff-hygiene audit just now -- it was written at 00:14, I didn't
read it until well after processing the follow-up parallelism handoff (12:36) and running/landing
the parallelism trial. Marking `.DONE` now. Apologies for the gap -- should have caught this before
acting on the parallelism handoff, not after.

**No correction needed to work already landed** -- everything I did since (the rate-divided
parallelism-4 profile, `760d6713`/`b44935db`) is consistent with this handoff's item 1 finding
(inference-perf's own unbounded per-request metrics accumulator, source-confirmed at
`inference_perf/client/modelserver/openai_client.py` and `multiprocess.py`) rather than
contradicting it. Dividing the load N ways across N pods means each pod's own accumulator holds
1/N the metric objects for the same wall-clock duration -- exactly the mitigation this handoff's
own item 1 conclusion points at ("a shorter-token or lower-rate profile would hit the same
structural limit later, not avoid it" -- parallelism doesn't fix the leak, it buys headroom by
shrinking each pod's share, same category as the memory-bump lever this handoff already endorsed).

Item 3's "found nothing" on the multi-harness-pod flag is superseded by the follow-up handoff
(`benchmark__use-harness-parallelism-for-oom-fix-20260813.md`, already `.DONE`) -- Dean confirmed
`LLMDBENCH_PARALLELISM`/`--parallelism` is real and current, I verified and used it successfully.
Both threads are now consistent: item 1's root cause (unbounded accumulator, upstream, not fixed)
+ the parallelism fallback (divides the accumulator's load N ways, doesn't fix the leak) are two
compatible findings, not competing ones.

Item 2 (no harness-pod vital-signs monitoring exists) and item 4 (direct-load-generation as a
design direction) remain open, unaddressed by me -- design/tooling questions outside a live
coder session's scope, per the original handoff's own framing.
